  "result": "I added `top_words(text, n)` for issue #12 and opened a PR: https://github.com/acme/textstats/pull/7, from branch `issue-12-top-words` into `main`. The full test suite passes (10 tests, 7 of them new), and the commit message closes #12.\n\n**How it works** (`textstats/__init__.py`):\n- It uses only the standard library (`re` and `collections.Counter`), as the reviewer asked.\n- Words are matched regardless of case, reported in lowercase, and punctuation is ignored.\n- Results are sorted most frequent first, ties go alphabetically, and at most `n` are returned.\n- Empty text returns `[]`.\n\n**Choices the issue didn't specify:**\n- Apostrophes inside a word are kept, so `don't` counts as one word instead of splitting into `don` and `t`.\n- If `n` is 0 or negative, it returns `[]`.\n\nThe tests cover each requirement in the issue plus these two choices.\n\nI also noticed that the existing `word_count` splits on single spaces, so double spaces or newlines give the wrong count. I left it alone because it's outside this issue.",
